00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.220
with the [INAUDIBLE]
partners, [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.170
network has rules, and so on.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.160
So it's a little bit hard.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.020
WePay offers easy-to-use APIs
and that completely abstract

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.800
away all the operations
difficulties,

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.430
the compliance difficulties,
and everything else.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.060
So, that's what WePay does.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.870
And here this is how
we are different.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.060
There are some
platforms [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.791
in the marketplace where they
give you some user experience

00:02:16.791 --> 00:02:17.290
control.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:22.080
So you can actually
integrate into their flows.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.160
The second case is they
offer risk protection,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.000
but none of them
actually do both--

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.290
giving you complete control
of the user experience

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.970
as well as user protection.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:38.180
So because we are
taking the risk,

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.040
that means our platform
has to be good.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.540
Just to give a sense of
what kind of risk we have--

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.367
because we are taking
payments from a known

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:52.760
party like a merchant,
we are taking payments

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.130
from the clients, and we
are paying out to merchants,

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.000
there could be
fraud on both sides.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.235
Merchants could be fraudulent,
the banks could be fraudulent,

00:03:01.235 --> 00:03:03.110
and sometimes there is
also collusion fraud--

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:07.130
where someone can play on
both sides of the offense.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.220
An example would be a bad
person could create a merchant

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.300
account, and have a bunch
of stolen credit cards,

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.939
and pay himself
all of the money.

00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:18.230
And then, after a while, the
real owner of those credit

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.380
cards will see that
hey, I don't really

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.460
pay this service provider.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.630
Why am I getting these charges?

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:25.910
So, they will charge back.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.250
The whole thing will
be unwound, and then we

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.020
will try to recoup
from the merchant.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.420
But much in this
[INAUDIBLE] is already gone,

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.590
so because we offer protection
we have to take the loss.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.630
Because of this the
risk management piece

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:39.890
is very important for us.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.490
So, because it is
very important let's

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:52.620
look at what makes
this platform great.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:53.960
Fraudsters are always changing.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.335
They are changing
their behavior,

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:57.010
they are testing their defenses.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:01.990
They are basically adapting
to the changes that we make.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.184
It's not like a one-time
one-directional move.

00:04:05.184 --> 00:04:05.850
It's like chess.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:06.730
We make a move.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:09.300
They make a move.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.820
But they are also leaking a
lot of data along the way.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.740
They are providing data
through the transactions.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.890
They are providing data
through environmental--

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.511
I won't get into the
exact details of that--

00:04:19.511 --> 00:04:21.010
but there is a lot
of data that they

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.800
leak in the kind of browsers
and other things that they use.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.780
There is also information
in terms of the vendor data.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.090
For instance, we can go
look at like third party

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.530
vendors like credit
bureaus and so

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.500
on-- what information that
they have, so a lot of data.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.320
We should be able to ingest
this diverse piece of data

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.700
and learn from it.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.500
We can't directly
throw in the raw data

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.500
and expect the models or
rules to perform well.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.170
We need to augment this
data as much as we can,

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.640
so that it becomes easier and
apparent for models and rules

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.717
to work on this data.

00:04:52.717 --> 00:04:54.550
And then we need to
have greater automation.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.050
We can't put all of these things
in front of the human agents

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.260
to--

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.530
basically, too much volume.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.410
It would be very much
cost-prohibitive to put

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.820
all of these cases in
front of the agents.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:11.680
So, what we need is
a high precision kind

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.680
of a system that captures
almost all the bad,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.632
but only touches
very few good people.

00:05:16.632 --> 00:05:17.840
That's what we want to build.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.610
Now to enable this to
convert the raw data

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:31.090
from the numbers like the price,
or the AVS response code, which

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:34.970
is the zip number that you
punch in at the gas stations,

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:35.470
and so on.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:38.890
So, we can augment
this data further

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:42.900
by adding geolocation
data, or we can aggregate

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.350
on multiple dimensions.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.570
Then it becomes
clear, hey, this user

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:51.055
has been only doing ASP of,
let's say $100, suddenly

00:05:51.055 --> 00:05:52.900
is doing a $5,000 one.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:53.910
It's risky.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.850
Models can easily
catch onto that,

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.610
and rules can also
easily trigger that.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:02.550
So, this is the toy example of
what we mean by single engine.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.020
We have the raw data, we
are the third party data

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.630
that we get from other
sources and so on, we

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:11.010
can augment this data with
the third party data, agreed

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.290
on any damages that
we want, and then

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.360
feed into the next
step of the pipeline.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.370
So that's the single
engine, that's

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.680
where we're going to
spend most of our time in

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.530
and how we build this
robust single engine.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:28.390
So, let's look at the goals of
such a system, so what should

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:30.690
it do for us.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.590
Given that the frost is
all this changing behavior,

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.370
we cannot know upfront all of
the things that we would want.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:42.022
For example, maybe they
are coming from certain IPs

00:06:42.022 --> 00:06:45.240
at some point, but they
will change their behavior.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:49.020
Maybe we can use
the ISP, we don't

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.070
know all the diameters
we want to agree

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.880
upon right in the beginning.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.530
Our platform should
be flexible enough

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:59.110
that without writing
any new code,

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:02.800
we should be able to
get these aggregations.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.220
The next thing is we don't
know what time frames make

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.210
sense for any specific problem.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.870
Certain businesses are
seasonal, so the season

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.260
could be quarterly.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:18.190
Some business are yearly, like
somebody selling umbrellas

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:19.834
in rainy season.

00:07:19.834 --> 00:07:21.250
That aspect, that
you don't expect

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:24.490
them to sell a bunch of
umbrellas in the summer.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:26.950
As a result, we should be
able to do year over year,

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:29.940
month over month, week
over week, year to date,

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.880
month to date, any
kind of aggregations

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:34.000
on arbitrary dimensions easily.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.130
Again, without adding new code.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:39.850
And then, these
events are happening

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:42.920
between the users'
lifecycle or payment itself,

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:47.110
like a payment has
authorized, it is captured,

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:49.580
it is not settled.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.250
Each of these users,
merchants, and payments

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.050
go through the life-cycles.

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:57.140
So they generate events,
and each of these events

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.990
will be injected
into our pipeline.

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:03.780
We want the latency to
be as small as possible.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.670
If we leave, let's say, a
five-minute or a 10-minute gap,

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:08.700
fraudsters will just
run through that.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.550
So, we don't want that.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:11.930
The ending processing
time should

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:15.650
be ideally in the
hundreds of milliseconds,

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.290
and at most of for our system
we said that one second is

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:22.210
the maximum we could tolerate.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:25.690
And then, it should
be easily scalable.

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:31.150
If the data is too small we can
do simple queries and group bys

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.890
and where clauses to
get out what you want.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.679
But once the data increases
and you want [INAUDIBLE],

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:39.070
those simple database
schemes don't work.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:42.729
We need a different
kind of solution.

00:08:42.729 --> 00:08:44.020
And it needs to be [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:48.550
Again, it should not be
DevOps-heavy and so on.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:51.280
Keeping those goals
in mind, we thought

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.100
of what is the abstract data
pipeline that will work for us.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:59.440
We talked about events getting
generated at the source.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.610
And we want them to go into some
kind of aggregation, which is

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:04.270
the black box on this stage--

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.675
we'll get into later
describing what it is--

00:09:06.675 --> 00:09:08.800
and we want it to generate
partial aggregates-- not

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.410
the real aggregates,
but partial aggregates.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.120
So, we can assemble
them in runtime

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:14.630
to decide what is
the right time,

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.000
and what is appropriate for us.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.320
And then we should have
some kind of interface

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:19.787
where users can say I like
this, but that I don't much

00:09:19.787 --> 00:09:22.120
care about, this is the kind
of thing that I care about,

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.920
and we get responses back.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.600
So, being a cloud native kind
of a company, [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:29.610
what's available at Google
stack to actually materialize

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.095
this in the real-world.

00:09:31.095 --> 00:09:32.470
Misha, can you
help us understand

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.270
what are the components
available for us

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:37.433
to make this happen?

00:09:37.433 --> 00:09:38.766
MISHA BRUKMAN: Thank you, Shyam.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:44.550
So before I get into the
details of the components

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.180
available on the
Google Cloud Platform,

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.300
let's talk a bit about
the history of Google

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.490
and where it comes from.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:53.052
So Google started off
to organize the world's

00:09:53.052 --> 00:09:54.510
information and
make it universally

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:55.690
accessible and useful.

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:58.350
And for that, Google built
the search application.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:09:59.850
Now the search
application obviously

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:01.860
has a lot of very
specific components

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:04.230
that are tuned in
geared for search.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.110
But even with that, there are
a number of generic big data

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.330
problems that
Google had to solve

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.130
to enable the search
application to run

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:13.620
at the scale of the internet.

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:15.409
For one, we needed
to be able to store

00:10:15.409 --> 00:10:17.700
the large amounts of data
that would go into the search

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:18.715
pipeline.

00:10:18.715 --> 00:10:21.090
And we needed to have different
types of storage systems,

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:22.590
depending on the
type of the content

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:23.760
that we would be storing.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.310
And that might involve
file systems, databases,

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.860
or other storage systems.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.081
Once we have that data, we
need to be able to process it,

00:10:32.081 --> 00:10:33.830
and we need to be able
to build the search

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:35.910
index from all of that content.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.220
That may take the shape of
either batch processing,

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.060
if we need to build the Link
Graph of the entire internet,

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.770
but we also need to have
real time stream processing

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:45.900
to capture all the updates
happening in real time

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.390
on web pages that change
every second, every minute.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:51.260
As opposed to the things
that stay constant and static

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.320
for months and years at a time.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.470
And finally, we need to
be able to serve this data

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:57.386
and serve this content,
and be able to upgrade

00:10:57.386 --> 00:10:59.757
our applications, such as
the search application,

00:10:59.757 --> 00:11:00.840
live without any downtime.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.370
A number of Google products have
reached over a billion users.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:11.450
So Search, Google Play, Chrome,
Android, YouTube, Maps, G-mail,

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:13.760
and Google Cloud
Platform all served

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:17.600
each over a billion users using
all the common infrastructure.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.820
And in fact, all of
these applications

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:22.790
and all of these services are
built on the same hardware,

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:25.760
the same networking, and
also the same software

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.480
stack that powers all of
these individual applications,

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.460
as well as available in
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:34.130
So, let's walk through
a couple of examples

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:35.990
of what are the software
applications that

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:38.705
make this possible across a
number of different domains.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:44.440
Over the years, Google has
built a number of technologies

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.770
to support all these
various service products.

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:51.430
Starting with the Google File
System, MapReduce, Bigtable,

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.696
Dremel, Colossus,
Spanner and others,

00:11:53.696 --> 00:11:55.570
Google has built a number
of generic systems,

00:11:55.570 --> 00:12:00.790
from storage to processing
to machine learning, as well

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:03.250
as container management, to
enable all these applications

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:04.630
to operate at scale.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.480
Despite their various
differences and their domains.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:10.610
And now, all of
these services are

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.130
available as part of Google
Cloud Platform grouped

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:16.280
into various areas, such as
compute, storage, databases,

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.184
big data, machine
learning and others.

00:12:18.184 --> 00:12:19.850
So, let's walk through
a couple examples

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:25.250
of these that maybe will be
useful for the team at WePay.

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:29.780
So for one, the question
is about serving our users

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:31.320
through the search application.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.130
So how do we run
containerized workloads such

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:36.770
that we can upgrade
and deploy and scale

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.200
all of our applications
very quickly and easily?

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.880
Google actually
wrote the support

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:42.440
for Containers and Linux--

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.180
an open source that
[INAUDIBLE] groups--

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:47.240
but we also needed something
to orchestrate the services

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.070
and to be able to upgrade them.

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:52.730
So we wrote Borg and then we
open sourced the Kubernetes

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:54.800
container manager system.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:57.860
And now we provide a managed
service around Kubernetes

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.510
as Google Container Engine.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:01.640
Container Engine provides
cluster management

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:02.510
and orchestration.

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:05.060
It provides a private
container registry

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.190
and it uses declarative
app resource specifications

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:09.590
that will enforce
for you at runtime.

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:15.010
It also provides auto scaling to
meet the needs of applications.

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:16.500
We also needed a
file system that

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:18.280
would be able to scale to
the size of the internet,

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:20.307
as well as grow with the
size of the internet.

00:13:20.307 --> 00:13:22.890
And we needed to store all the
data from the growing internet,

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:25.000
as well as all the other
applications that users

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.700
might generate.

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:29.040
So we first built
Google File System,

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.230
and then we upgraded
that with Colossus.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.820
And today we provide
Google Cloud Storage,

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:36.300
which is an exabyte-scale
file system that

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:37.460
has high throughput.

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:40.710
It has replicated through
regional and multi-regional

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:41.880
buckets.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:45.750
It has a uniform API across
various storage classes

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:48.870
so you can either store cold
data, warm data, or hot data

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:51.100
and access them uniformly
to the same API,

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:52.740
so you don't have
to change your code

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:55.510
to be able to access the
different types of storage.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.370
It also has an
HDFS-compatible connector,

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:59.370
so you can use that from
Hadoop without changing

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.014
your code at all.

00:14:02.014 --> 00:14:03.430
Once we have all
this data we need

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:05.170
to be able to process
the data scale,

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:07.170
and as I mentioned we
need to be able to process

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:10.724
the data both in batch mode
as well as streaming mode.

00:14:10.724 --> 00:14:12.140
Over the years,
Google has written

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:15.390
a number of different systems
such as MapReduce, Flume,

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:17.790
and Millwheel, and we've
combined all of those

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.310
into Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.550
Then what we did was we open
sourced the SDK behind Google

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.780
Cloud Dataflow as Apache Beam.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:28.230
Apache Beam recently
became a top-level project

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.470
in the patches
software foundation.

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:33.320
And you can run code that
reduces the Apache Beam's

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:36.030
SDK on to of other execution
engines besides just Google

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:37.180
Cloud Dataflow.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:40.520
You can also use it
on SPARC and Flink.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:42.880
Dataflow, as I
mentioned, combines

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:44.779
both of the models,
both page and streaming,

00:14:44.779 --> 00:14:46.570
so that you don't have
to rewrite your code

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:47.819
to go from batch to streaming.

00:14:47.819 --> 00:14:50.135
The same exact code
will work in both modes.

00:14:50.135 --> 00:14:51.760
So you have instant
portability and you

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:53.950
can make the decision of
how to deploy the code.

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:58.090
We'll see later how WePay
uses that to their advantage.

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:00.770
Dataflow provides auto scaling
and dynamic task charting,

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:03.830
so you don't have the problem
of the long stragglers taking

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:06.700
a very long time and slowing
down your overall process.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:10.090
When Dataflow notices that
the tasks are taking too long,

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:12.430
it will break up the
tasks, reassign the work,

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:14.290
and spin off additional
workers if it

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:17.140
needs to make sure that
your jobs complete quickly.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:20.830
Dataflow provides connectors
to various storage systems,

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.420
such as Bigtable,
BigQuery, and Cloud

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:25.240
Service, who can read and write
from anywhere to anywhere.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.670
And as I mentioned, it is
portable with the Apache Beam

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:28.170
SDK.

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:33.590
So, we also needed a way to run
open source processing engines,

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:34.880
such as Hadoop and SPARC.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:38.590
And so for that, we wrote and
provide Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:42.290
Dataproc provides Hadoop, SPARC,
Pig, Hive and other big data

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:43.320
components.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.340
It also provides connectors
to Bigtable, Bigquery,

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:47.670
and Cloud Storage--

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:51.260
what this allows you to do
is that you can run stateless

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:52.550
Hadoop and SPARC clusters.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:54.230
This means that you can
bring them up very quickly

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:56.396
and turn them down when you
need when you don't need

00:15:56.396 --> 00:15:59.090
to use them, because
you are using Bigtable,

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:03.110
Cloud Storage, and Bigquery
as hosts of storage solutions.

00:16:03.110 --> 00:16:05.330
This also allows you to do
something very different.

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:08.450
It allows you to think about
your processing as jobs,

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:10.371
rather than think about
long-lived clusters.

00:16:10.371 --> 00:16:12.370
So, rather than saying,
I need to run a cluster,

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:13.850
I need to maintain
a cluster, you

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:16.850
can just submit jobs using
the Cloud Dataproc API.

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:19.550
It will make those clusters
appear, it will grow them,

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:21.105
and you can then turn them down.

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:26.420
In addition to
the batch storage,

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:30.880
we also needed very fast
storage at terabyte scale.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:33.040
So what happens
specifically with Search,

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:35.020
is that some pages,
as I mentioned,

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:38.020
get updated very, very quickly
while others take days or weeks

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:39.670
or months to be updated.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:42.070
If we always build our
indexes in batch mode

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:45.670
by rewriting large index
files, we will always be behind

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:48.010
and we will have a very
stale search index.

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:50.190
So, what we needed
was a database

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:51.970
that scaled to
the petabytes that

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:54.580
gave us random access to
every single data item

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.020
in the database
very, very quickly.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:58.900
And so for that,
we built Bigtable.

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:00.650
And now that's available
as Google Cloud--

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:02.524
Bigtable is a part of
Google Cloud Platform--

00:17:02.524 --> 00:17:04.720
and I'll go into a
bit more detail about

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:08.310
what is Bigtable, how it works,
and what it makes possible.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.369
So Bigtable is a NoSQL database.

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:11.920
It's a fully managed service.

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:15.339
It provides petabyte-scale in
storage with low latency access

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:16.960
to any item in the database.

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:19.270
It provides seamless
scalability for throughput,

00:17:19.270 --> 00:17:22.599
and it learns and adjusts
to the access patterns.

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:26.410
Let's do a deep dive
into the last two points.

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:30.610
So the Bigtable schema provides
a sparse, NoSQL white column

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:31.730
database.

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:34.420
What this means is that
if you're not storing data

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:38.030
in some of the cells, that does
not count against the storage.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:40.620
Bigtable provides a single
index, which is a row key.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:43.830
So, row key design is very
important when using Bigtable.

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:46.000
And it provides atomic
single row transactions

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.460
across all of the columns.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:49.960
The best way to
think about Bigtable

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.030
is as a three-dimensional
spreadsheet.

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:55.060
There are rows,
columns, and every cell

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:57.130
has time-stamped versions.

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:58.870
Now, those versions
are in 64, so you

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:00.280
can consider them as time.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:02.500
You can also use them as
arbitrary version IDs,

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:04.240
so each of those
intersections there

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:05.440
can have multiple versions.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:11.620
At a high level, clients
connect to a single endpoint

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.470
which then dispatches
the request

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.647
to the various Bigtable nodes.

00:18:15.647 --> 00:18:17.230
In the next couple
of slides I'm going

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:20.597
to remove the load balancing
and proxy layer for simplicity,

00:18:20.597 --> 00:18:22.180
to show the actual
connections and how

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:26.350
they will be forwarded from the
client to the processing nodes.

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:28.930
But always remember that
there is that load balancing

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:31.270
proxy layer, so that
clients don't actually

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:33.940
have to have that discovery
service to find out

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:36.170
which node they need
to connect to and when.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:42.650
One interesting
thing about Bigtable

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:44.900
is that Bigtable, even
though it's a database,

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:46.910
is actually a stateless system.

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:49.516
So Bigtable doesn't
actually store any data.

00:18:49.516 --> 00:18:51.140
All the data that is
stored in Bigtable

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:54.950
is actually stored on Colossus,
our distributed file system.

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:58.140
This is actually true for all
other Google Storage systems.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:00.980
This is something that
Bigtable, Spanner, and Bigquery

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:01.520
all share.

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:07.910
So, let's say we have a
particular table node that

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:10.550
is managing a bunch
of data on Colossus,

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:12.740
and all of a sudden, the
data that it's managing

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:14.880
is becoming popular
with the clients,

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:16.520
and so they keep
all accessing it.

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:19.730
So now that no it is getting
more activity and more requests

00:19:19.730 --> 00:19:21.830
than other nearby
nodes, Bigtable

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:24.050
will notice this
and automatically

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:28.160
re-balance the data such
that the load will then

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:31.400
spread to other nodes, then it
will even the flow distribution

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:32.570
on the cluster.

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:34.010
This happens transparently.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:35.480
The clients are
not aware of this.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.813
As I mentioned earlier, they
go through a load balancing

00:19:37.813 --> 00:19:40.190
layer that proxies
these requests

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:41.727
to the different nodes.

00:19:41.727 --> 00:19:44.060
Now, what happens here is
that the data doesn't actually

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:47.120
move from a Bigtable node
to another Bigtable node.

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:50.630
The data stays continuously
in the Colossus file system.

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:52.390
All that happens is
that we're resigning

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:54.770
metadata of the
assignment of the data

00:19:54.770 --> 00:19:56.060
from one node to another.

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:58.130
So this is actually a
very fast operation.

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:03.460
It does not take size of data,
it takes seconds or less.

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:06.220
What it also allows
us to do is that it

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:10.610
allows us to scale the cluster,
both up and down, to provide

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:12.410
more throughput to the data.

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:14.510
Because re-assignment
is really quick,

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:16.760
what this means is that we
can add more and more nodes

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:19.130
to the cluster, and they
will share and split

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:22.330
the responsibility for the
data management in Colossus.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:23.330
And it will be seamless.

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:24.788
Again, the clients
will not notice.

00:20:24.788 --> 00:20:26.870
They will just get
higher throughput

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:30.080
to the underlying data.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:33.790
What this does provide us, then,
is because the nodes are able

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:36.619
to quickly reassign and
manage data separately,

00:20:36.619 --> 00:20:38.660
and the nodes don't
communicate with each other--

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:40.660
there is a background
process behind the scenes,

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:42.670
which is the Bigtable
master node which manages

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:43.840
the assignments--

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:47.530
this provides us with linear
scalability in throughput

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:48.730
to Bigtable.

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:53.380
So, whether you have three nodes
in your cluster, 30, or 300,

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:56.860
the processing power is directly
proportional to the number

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:57.640
of nodes.

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:01.090
This allows for linear
scalability and performance.

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:04.780
Many storage systems tend to
flatten out and then limit

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:07.360
the scalability as the
cluster size grows.

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:08.950
Not so the Bigtable.

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:11.590
In March of last year,
we presented a use case

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:15.350
where a customer used 3,500
Bigtable nodes with 200 data

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:17.740
flow workers of 32
cores each, and it also

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:19.120
scales linearly at that scale.

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:22.200
And we've tested it with larger
clusters beyond that as well.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.820
At this point, I would
like to turn it over to Wei

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:27.530
from WePay to continue the
conversation about what

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:28.030
they used.

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:33.112
WEI: Thanks, Misha.

00:21:33.112 --> 00:21:34.490
Hi, everybody.

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:35.640
My name is Wei Li.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:39.140
I am the engineer leading
the data aggregation pipeline

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:39.690
at WePlay.

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:45.270
In the section of
my talk, I'm going

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:48.880
to go some technical
dive into how we set up

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:52.530
the data pipeline, the
challenges that they are

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:55.530
facing, the
architectural decisions

00:21:55.530 --> 00:22:00.720
that are made, and
also show the problems

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:05.340
that we deploy to production,
and I will share with you what

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:08.162
the key take aways are
from is our implementation.

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:16.130
So let's come back to
the technical goals.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:19.310
When we start, we want to create
a data pipeline essentially

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:21.320
that can aggregate
on any dimension,

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:25.700
and that can answer problems
for arbitrary time windows.

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:27.560
These are the feature request--

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:29.870
actually, we are going to
go into more detail when

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:31.730
we do in the implementation--

00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:35.750
and the next three is about
the end-to-end processing

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:37.920
time, less than one second.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:39.440
So, this is more
like a performance.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:42.260
We did some upfront
testing and we're

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:48.420
going to have more in the
architecture analysis as well.

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:53.124
And the last tutorial comment
is why is the easy scaling?

00:22:53.124 --> 00:22:54.790
Because, we are facing
the issue that we

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:56.900
are having right now--
the old database system

00:22:56.900 --> 00:23:01.190
can not hold up the traffic,
and easy maintainability.

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:06.640
So, this is crucial for us to
go to a large-scale production.

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:09.410
But, engineer-wise, it is
actually pretty challenging,

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:11.750
because when we are talking
about easy scaling, which

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:13.850
means that we want
a system which

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:16.940
can go [INAUDIBLE]
at the same time

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:18.780
that our traffic is growing.

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:22.790
So, the easy
maintainability that anybody

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:25.460
has the experience to
get the data plan set up,

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:31.010
we know that it probably would
involve batch and streaming,

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:34.410
which it's really
hard to maintain.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:39.550
Those are hard technical
questions and WePay is actually

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:40.280
a start-up.

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:43.310
We found a better
resource, but our business

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:46.940
is growing, so we want to
get the system ready fast.

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:48.890
This is where Google
comes into the picture.

00:23:51.704 --> 00:23:53.120
I brought a slide
from [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:56.360
to look at different
aspects for the competition.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:59.630
So, we are going to
look into this choice

00:23:59.630 --> 00:24:03.440
and combined with
our WePay set-up

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:07.580
to see what we can do
to set up and create

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:10.310
converting the abstract
pipeline to a more

00:24:10.310 --> 00:24:13.930
concrete implementation.

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:16.180
Here is the slide
that we had earlier

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:20.890
for the abstract architecture
for real-time pipeline.

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:23.170
Potentially we have four
components-- the streaming

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:27.430
source, aggregation engine, and
partial aggregation storage,

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:30.530
and the customer query UI.

00:24:30.530 --> 00:24:32.920
So, in this I will go
component by component

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:35.320
to see why we make
the decision we made,

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:38.270
and which component
we picked to use.

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:39.820
So first off, the
streaming source.

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:45.900
We actually look at two
different components--

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:48.960
Apache Kafka and Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:51.000
We started with
the Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:55.110
We did a performance analysis,
which preformed well.

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:59.990
But WePay is actually using
a lot of Apache Kafka inside

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:02.870
of our system overall
technology stack,

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:05.130
and these are widely available.

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:08.480
So, even though
the two components

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.960
have similar
functional features,

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:16.490
WePay's ecosystem is more
inclined to be Apache Kafla So

00:25:16.490 --> 00:25:22.230
that's why we will eventually
end up using the Apache Kafka.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:26.190
So, the final outcome
is that we're actually

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:29.250
hosting the Apache
Kafka using the computer

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:33.110
engine inside Google Cloud.

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:36.810
The next component that comes in
mind is the aggregation engine.

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:39.480
So, the component we look
at is the Cloud Dataflow.

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:47.460
There are a lot of good stuff
on the Dataflow features,

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:50.130
which is supposedly
easy to support the data

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:52.410
transformation, and
more important is

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:55.950
that it has a unified model
for both streaming and a batch

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:57.450
processing.

00:25:57.450 --> 00:25:59.460
Which is actually
really important for us

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:02.790
when we think about
this architecture, when

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:05.370
we are trying to make
it more operational.

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:09.330
And another outstanding
feature is that it

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:11.700
handles the runtime processing.

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:17.310
Which means that we actually
can get determinacy behavior

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:20.730
every time we
process the events.

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:24.550
Also, the strong "exactly-once"
semantics is really important

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:29.040
for us so that we don't
duplicate the events.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:32.970
But, most importantly
is that this is actually

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:34.480
a manager services.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:37.450
It scales well horizontally, so
it fits in our technical goals

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:40.580
that we can scale well.

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:43.640
That's actually our
technical choice to us

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:46.200
the Dataflow as the
aggregation engine.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:49.080
The next component we have
is the partial aggregation

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:49.580
storage.

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.770
The component we will look
at is the Cloud Bigtable.

00:26:54.770 --> 00:26:56.840
As Misha already
said, it is proving

00:26:56.840 --> 00:27:00.950
to be handled petabyte
data scale use case,

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:05.480
and also very low latency in
terms of both read and write.

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:08.150
And it has a lot of
integration with Dataflow.

00:27:08.150 --> 00:27:11.060
So, it's a Bigtable IO is there.

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:13.250
And for our business
questions, if you

00:27:13.250 --> 00:27:15.860
want to [INAUDIBLE]
serious [INAUDIBLE],

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:17.860
we have a proper schema design.

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:20.000
It handles it really well.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:23.000
And, most important is that
it is also a manager service,

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:27.340
it can scale well horizontally.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:29.710
The last component
that we're looking at

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:33.080
is the customer query UI.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:36.650
This is actually
our business logic.

00:27:36.650 --> 00:27:40.910
We obviously decided on building
a REST Java service running

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:43.150
using these Docker containers.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:45.680
The goal of this
REST query UI is just

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:49.550
acting as data query
proxy for the Bigtable

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:52.910
to embody our query logic, for
example, the custom stitching

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:57.050
logic to answer different
custom interval questions.

00:27:57.050 --> 00:28:00.570
And given that the
services is stateless,

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:03.900
we can actually scale
well really easily just

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:08.490
by bumping up the replication
to multiple instances.

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:10.570
So all these
considerations, this

00:28:10.570 --> 00:28:12.690
comes down to our
concrete architecture

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:14.480
for real-time pipeline.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:19.610
So as you can see
in the diagram here,

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:21.950
we actually instrumented
all our application code

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:25.940
and collected events when
the events happening and then

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:32.540
pump into Apache Kafka, and
has the KafkaIO streaming

00:28:32.540 --> 00:28:35.210
the events to the Dataflow
processing engine,

00:28:35.210 --> 00:28:37.180
and then with the
Apache aggregation

00:28:37.180 --> 00:28:40.150
we just periodically
output it in the Bigtable.

00:28:40.150 --> 00:28:43.400
And then we have
some ways to query

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:46.400
that-- to answer our business
questions for the data

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:49.820
consumers to use.

00:28:49.820 --> 00:28:52.960
So the next step come with
what to anticipate if we

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:56.180
want to get this in production.

00:28:56.180 --> 00:29:01.080
So in production, failure
is always happening.

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:05.570
We need to be able to handle
them and to be resilient.

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:10.010
And if you only use one
single-stream pipeline,

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:11.950
it's not robust enough.

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:14.120
So the natural
thinking is to come

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:17.190
to a mixed stream and batch.

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:19.400
And this actually fits
really well the Dataflow.

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:22.410
That's why our original
thought of why we used this one

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:27.760
is that they have a unified
processing model for both.

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:30.320
The challenge for us is
that KafkaIO by default

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:33.990
is streaming IO, so if
we want to set up a batch

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:37.680
we need to do some
customization on this one.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:41.570
So the good thing about us is
that our events are actually

00:29:41.570 --> 00:29:44.630
all time-based, so
for all the events

00:29:44.630 --> 00:29:48.590
we actually mandatory to have a
custom event when it happened.

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:52.990
So that's the base for us
to create a bounded KafkaIO.

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:54.770
So look at this
example, this example

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:59.590
is assuming that we
have six events come in,

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:03.310
and all the green ones are
coming in over the same hour,

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:06.130
and we have some other
events coming in.

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:08.720
And in the production
in our department,

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:10.300
we actually have
an hourly batch job

00:30:10.300 --> 00:30:13.420
which actually aggregates
in the hourly interval.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:18.580
So for that particular case,
our customized bounded KafkaIO,

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:22.380
what it does is it will
take the range for the hour

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:24.160
that it's interested
to aggregate

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:26.985
and then read through all
the Kafka events up to now,

00:30:26.985 --> 00:30:31.210
and only output the
events [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:33.490
your interested range.

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:38.440
So by doing this, we essentially
achieve the batch processing.

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:42.507
And with this last
problem solved,

00:30:42.507 --> 00:30:44.590
we actually go through the
full-fledged production

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:48.130
deployment with all these
kinds of variations.

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:51.120
So as you can see, this
diagram is that in the center

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:52.300
it's still one line.

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:56.380
We have streaming, processing,
persisting, and the query,

00:30:56.380 --> 00:30:59.430
but we have different flavors
of pipeline processing.

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:02.255
On top is real-time streaming,
which actually consists

00:31:02.255 --> 00:31:05.700
of listening to the Kafka events
and persisting periodically

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:09.640
to the Bigtable, in the finest
granularity, I think for us,

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:11.380
it's using minutes.

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:13.150
And also for
different granularity

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:17.350
we have the hourly batch,
daily batch, and other batch.

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.360
So given that we have
the custom bounded

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:23.090
KafkaIO setup, so
they just take events

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:24.660
and we just deploy events.

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:27.940
It's using one single code base,
and it is really easy for us

00:31:27.940 --> 00:31:29.440
to maintain and manage.

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:35.500
So then with this
production [INAUDIBLE],

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:39.392
we can come back to visit
our technical goals.

00:31:39.392 --> 00:31:41.414
I'll go about the first two.

00:31:41.414 --> 00:31:43.080
You'll see the last
three we have talked

00:31:43.080 --> 00:31:45.167
about why we make the decision.

00:31:45.167 --> 00:31:47.250
So the first two is figuring
out, but why is that?

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:50.430
We want to do a dynamic
aggregation or any dimensions

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:51.920
using our aggregation engine.

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:57.880
The first illustration for us is
that we are thinking just, OK,

00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.200
every time we want a new
aggregation we just stand up

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:02.130
a new custom data
pipeline [INAUDIBLE],

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.470
but they are doing
customized transformation

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:06.870
and doing aggregation.

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:09.750
Actually that turned out
to not be very flexible,

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:14.670
because if you want to add one
new metrics, new aggregation,

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:16.841
you want to deploy a new
pipeline, that's too hard,

00:32:16.841 --> 00:32:17.340
right?

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:19.480
Operationally it's not feasible.

00:32:19.480 --> 00:32:22.230
So what we end up is actually
create a linear transformation,

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:23.700
it's very simple.

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:28.680
So the [INAUDIBLE] is kind of
linear, so for the first time

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:30.950
we just read all
the events coming,

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:33.770
and then we just [INAUDIBLE]
window by different custom

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:37.560
event times, and then
group by custom dimension,

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:40.050
and aggregate admins
inside the same group,

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:43.530
and output a pure record
into the Bigtable.

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:49.350
So on the right, as
you can see there's a--

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:51.630
This is actually our
testing case example.

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:54.990
So this is just one
linear transformation

00:32:54.990 --> 00:32:58.520
of the DAG of our
Dataflow pipeline.

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:01.340
And what's flexible
about this is

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:04.730
that we actually make the
schema kind of flexible.

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:08.120
So for example, we have
four required texts

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:09.470
for every schema.

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:11.840
So first one is the timestamp.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:14.655
This is mandatory, so
every event has a time--

00:33:14.655 --> 00:33:18.095
that's the base for us to
play by the event time--

00:33:18.095 --> 00:33:21.880
and also has a
customized dimension.

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:27.110
You can provide your own
dimension by filling this tag.

00:33:27.110 --> 00:33:30.710
And also we support
custom types like numeric,

00:33:30.710 --> 00:33:32.170
which for numerical
type could be

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:34.110
able to collect what's
the minimum, what's

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:37.970
the average, what's the
maximum, and total, and count

00:33:37.970 --> 00:33:40.400
[INAUDIBLE], so it's kind
of additional aggregation

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:41.280
on this one.

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:43.970
Also categorical, so
we're going to give you

00:33:43.970 --> 00:33:47.650
back the unique values count
for this particular window

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:48.980
dimension.

00:33:48.980 --> 00:33:50.930
And also the
geolocation is actually

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:53.160
used that in this
particular window

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:55.890
was the geo-span of
the events happening.

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:58.410
So this is custom
types that we provided.

00:33:58.410 --> 00:34:01.910
And for the value field is just
a serialized form of the value

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:06.510
data we can custom combine
and will consume and generate.

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:11.469
Let's look at one example
how we make this work.

00:34:11.469 --> 00:34:16.560
So one event we are
seeing is, for example--

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:18.130
Let's use a fake event.

00:34:18.130 --> 00:34:20.500
So this is a
SuperABC store which

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:22.659
received a $100
payment originated

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:31.530
from Redwood City, CA,
94063, on Wednesday, March 1.

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:34.860
So the highlight place actually
represent different attributes

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:36.090
of this event.

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:37.940
So one event we can
extract from this one

00:34:37.940 --> 00:34:40.320
is the payment amount, right?

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:42.724
So we can generate events.

00:34:42.724 --> 00:34:48.330
For this is at this time the
dimension of payment amount,

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:53.460
we create a numeric
metric which is $100.

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:56.120
And after our
aggregation pipeline,

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:58.690
we got the result, we can
answer the question like,

00:34:58.690 --> 00:35:01.760
what are the total processed
payment amount you're seeing?

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:04.080
And how about average amount
and the standard deviation

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:05.160
in that sector?

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:10.020
So by doing this, this can
do the [INAUDIBLE] detection.

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:11.970
From the same event,
we can also generate

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:14.790
another metric which is like,
for example, the payment

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:15.650
location.

00:35:15.650 --> 00:35:20.610
Then we can just easily change
the type to the geolocation

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:23.190
and give you the value.

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:25.620
And with this can say
what's the geo-distribution

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:28.650
of your payments for
particular merchant.

00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:34.590
So by combining these
two we have a simple DAG,

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:37.320
which means that we have
a very stable pipeline

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:39.520
and we have a
flexible schema, which

00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:41.020
means that we can
dynamically adjust

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:42.680
to adding any aggregations.

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:44.680
So if you're interested,
for example, like Shyam

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:47.590
said earlier, for us to
change their patterns.

00:35:47.590 --> 00:35:48.980
And if we see any
new aggregation

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:52.840
and we are interested, we
just pump in different things.

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:54.800
So when we combine
these two together,

00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:57.660
we've got a very stable
and still very flexible

00:35:57.660 --> 00:36:00.480
solution that can support
our fast iteration

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:04.960
on the aggregation on
different dimensions.

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:07.630
So the next challenge
is for us to come down

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:10.270
to the time-series queries.

00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:13.210
So one thing we
leverage from Bigtable

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:15.280
is to use the row key design.

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:19.450
So we set up the row keys so
that the timestamp is always

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:23.057
the first step part
of the row key.

00:36:23.057 --> 00:36:25.390
So essentially, if you are
interested in particular time

00:36:25.390 --> 00:36:29.690
range, you can filter by the
prefix and get results back.

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:35.380
And also for performance
optimization,

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:37.840
we actually create
different column families

00:36:37.840 --> 00:36:39.580
for defined granularity.

00:36:39.580 --> 00:36:43.000
For example, an example here
we have minutes, hour, day,

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:43.720
and week.

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:48.920
So then when we're trying
to answer the question

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:51.390
for the custom time interval.

00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:55.520
So one naive solution is that if
we just go through every minute

00:36:55.520 --> 00:36:57.180
and combine them together.

00:36:57.180 --> 00:36:58.880
So in the example
here, if we want

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:03.200
to aggregate between
February 25 to March 8,

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:06.080
if you go by minute level,
it's too many, right?

00:37:06.080 --> 00:37:08.480
It's not optimized, given
that we have defined

00:37:08.480 --> 00:37:10.460
granularly aggregation already.

00:37:10.460 --> 00:37:13.940
So we actually implement
this time stitching,

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:17.660
in the sense that we can guess
the values from the largest

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:21.090
granularity first, and then
extend to the next level,

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:21.980
and so on.

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:25.250
So we can get all the values,
but we can reduce the load

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:26.684
to the Bigtable.

00:37:26.684 --> 00:37:28.100
I'm sure Bigtable
can handle this,

00:37:28.100 --> 00:37:30.080
but we just don't
want to overload it.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:36.220
So the next technical
goal is the performance

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:37.600
and maintainability.

00:37:37.600 --> 00:37:42.510
So we have been able to deploy
the pipeline into production

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:46.360
for several months and it's
actually performed pretty well

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:48.070
compared to the old system.

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:51.610
It's also very stable and fast.

00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:55.360
And in summary, is that
the key takeaway for us

00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:59.940
is that Google Cloud managed
service actually shields us

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:03.290
from those heavy lifting
technical challenges

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:05.780
such as scaling out and
dynamic resource management,

00:38:05.780 --> 00:38:09.590
et cetera, that we can
actually put more energy

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:11.550
and focus on the
business problems, that's

00:38:11.550 --> 00:38:13.880
the beauty of the
infrastructure.

00:38:16.700 --> 00:38:19.630
So and also inside
WePay we are using

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:22.110
lots of components
provided by Google Cloud

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:26.380
Platform such as BigQuery,
Pub/Sub, Datastore.

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:31.050
And we have a pretty good post
on it at wecode.wepay.com,

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:34.490
so if you're interested
you can check it out.

00:38:34.490 --> 00:38:37.010
And last, I would
like to really thank

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:42.160
the Google Cloud teams,
both Bigtable, Dataflow,

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:44.510
the support.

00:38:44.510 --> 00:38:48.950
During the process, [INAUDIBLE]
with various teams, and they're

00:38:48.950 --> 00:38:52.490
really helpful and they
help us get the pipelines up

00:38:52.490 --> 00:38:54.000
and running.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:56.420
And last not least, I'd also
like to thank all the team

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:58.010
members from WePay.

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:00.440
This is not just one
project by development,

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:03.380
it's actually joint
between the operational

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:04.760
and the development.

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:06.710
If your managed
pipelines really hard,

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:10.640
and both teams are working
really closely to make this

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:11.890
happen.

00:39:11.890 --> 00:39:15.020
So with this said, I'll
hand over the mic to Robin

00:39:15.020 --> 00:39:15.770
from Outbound.

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:16.270
Thanks.

00:39:23.829 --> 00:39:24.870
ROBIN TANG: Hi, everyone.

00:39:24.870 --> 00:39:29.086
My name is Robin and I'm the
lead engineer at Outbound.

00:39:29.086 --> 00:39:30.460
Today I'll be
talking to you guys

00:39:30.460 --> 00:39:32.740
about on-demand
scaling with Bigtable

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:35.190
to achieve low latency
and high throughput.

00:39:38.480 --> 00:39:40.760
So first, we started
about four years ago,

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:43.310
and currently we process
about 40 million events,

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:47.030
and off of that one million
messages are sent per day.

00:39:47.030 --> 00:39:49.380
We started with a
very simple question--

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:52.400
what if you could create and
manage any kind of message

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:54.410
without engineering help?

00:39:54.410 --> 00:39:56.690
Going off of that, what
if you could measure

00:39:56.690 --> 00:39:59.030
the quality of each
message by seeing what

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:01.200
users do after they receive it?

00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:07.000
When I mean any kind of
message, I truly mean any kind.

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:10.630
Outbound supports email,
SMS, mobile, web push,

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:12.462
and even webhooks for
advanced use cases

00:40:12.462 --> 00:40:13.670
that we have not covered yet.

00:40:16.550 --> 00:40:19.880
After we send a message,
there's much more to it

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:23.570
than who opened the message
or who clicked the link.

00:40:23.570 --> 00:40:25.400
It's more about
the user's journey.

00:40:25.400 --> 00:40:26.990
What did they do
inside of your app

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:28.850
after they receive the message?

00:40:28.850 --> 00:40:31.730
For instance, does a
churn-reducing drip actually

00:40:31.730 --> 00:40:32.610
lead to less churn?

00:40:35.920 --> 00:40:37.990
Outbound allows you
to do three things.

00:40:37.990 --> 00:40:41.190
First, decide who you should
message by segmentation

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:43.800
on user actions and attributes.

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:47.670
Second, help you convey your
message with a powerful editor

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:49.770
and multichannel support.

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:52.020
And third, look
at the report page

00:40:52.020 --> 00:40:54.930
and see which variant
is doing better or worse

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:59.770
and adjust the
relocation allocations.

00:40:59.770 --> 00:41:03.080
So here's an example of our
segmentation in process.

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:06.640
First, we want to message users
living in San Francisco or Palo

00:41:06.640 --> 00:41:09.760
Alto or Mountain View,
and we want to message

00:41:09.760 --> 00:41:11.710
those who have given
us their phone numbers,

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:14.960
so the phone number
exists in our database.

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:19.270
Third, we want to message
those who are on a trial,

00:41:19.270 --> 00:41:22.280
so account type
is equal to trial.

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:24.260
Our filters are
Boolean expressions

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:27.270
and we allow users to
chain on ANDs and ORs.

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:32.040
Here you're able to
pick the type of channel

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:33.462
you want to message on.

00:41:33.462 --> 00:41:34.920
And if you're not
sure, you're also

00:41:34.920 --> 00:41:37.020
able to create more
variants in the A/B test

00:41:37.020 --> 00:41:38.390
on different channels.

00:41:38.390 --> 00:41:41.160
Say for example, you want to
test on mobile versus web push.

00:41:45.370 --> 00:41:47.800
Once you have picked a channel
and populated the message

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:50.350
content, you're able to
define any goals that you

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:52.030
want Outbound to track.

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:54.940
So in this example, we want
users to read more blog posts

00:41:54.940 --> 00:41:58.120
within one day of
receiving this message,

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:00.970
and we want users to make
more orders within three

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:02.275
days of receiving this message.

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:07.570
Here you're able
to see a reporting

00:42:07.570 --> 00:42:09.670
page summarizing three things.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:13.570
One, number of unique message
users touched by this campaign.

00:42:13.570 --> 00:42:15.740
Two, different
variant statistics,

00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:17.530
including control groups.

00:42:17.530 --> 00:42:19.780
And three, any goals
you may have set up.

00:42:23.620 --> 00:42:26.410
On our backend, we see
about 2,000 to 4,000 events

00:42:26.410 --> 00:42:29.560
per second, and it grows
10% month over month.

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:32.380
Our peak to baseline
ratio is four to 10

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:34.710
because we have customers
internationally.

00:42:34.710 --> 00:42:37.720
So as to be able to sustain
this type of usage pattern,

00:42:37.720 --> 00:42:41.470
we either need to over-provision
or set up auto-scaling.

00:42:41.470 --> 00:42:46.120
Also, per API call, the
workload variance is very high.

00:42:46.120 --> 00:42:49.030
It could range from backfilling
a user's history to triggering

00:42:49.030 --> 00:42:52.620
campaigns and goals.

00:42:52.620 --> 00:42:55.490
Now let's look at a diagram
of our backend infrastructure.

00:42:55.490 --> 00:42:58.880
So when an API call comes in,
it hits our load balancer,

00:42:58.880 --> 00:43:00.470
then spins off to
one of our servers

00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:02.990
that does minimal validation.

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:05.360
Such validations can
include, does a track call

00:43:05.360 --> 00:43:06.545
have an event on it?

00:43:06.545 --> 00:43:08.560
And does it have a
user ID attached?

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:11.840
And whether the API
key is valid or not.

00:43:11.840 --> 00:43:13.640
Once this has been
validated, it then

00:43:13.640 --> 00:43:16.700
gets put into one of our
various queues and workers

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:18.610
pull items off of
various queues.

00:43:22.520 --> 00:43:24.170
When marketers come
in, they usually

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:26.150
have a goal that
they want a lift in.

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:28.430
They would typically
pipe their event stream

00:43:28.430 --> 00:43:32.930
into analytics products such as
Mixpanel or Google Analytics.

00:43:32.930 --> 00:43:34.760
Then off of those
analytics products

00:43:34.760 --> 00:43:37.700
they will try to generate
predictive indicators.

00:43:37.700 --> 00:43:39.500
With those indicators
in hand they

00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:41.750
will run tests to
validate it and tune

00:43:41.750 --> 00:43:43.340
wherever it's necessary.

00:43:43.340 --> 00:43:45.290
This process is
often very lengthy

00:43:45.290 --> 00:43:47.595
and requires a lot of
engineering help and support.

00:43:50.930 --> 00:43:53.400
Outbound is now able
to improve this process

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:55.740
by generating ideas
for the marketer using

00:43:55.740 --> 00:43:58.350
their own accounts event stream.

00:43:58.350 --> 00:44:01.050
The market now only needs
to approve the suggestion

00:44:01.050 --> 00:44:04.260
and can then go on to write
the content without being

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:05.788
blocked by engineering.

00:44:08.660 --> 00:44:11.680
So this is an example of a
model for a video subscription

00:44:11.680 --> 00:44:12.730
company.

00:44:12.730 --> 00:44:15.760
The goal here is to
add more subscription.

00:44:15.760 --> 00:44:18.940
So the insight is users who
verify email and then don't

00:44:18.940 --> 00:44:21.100
read an article
within a day are less

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:23.570
likely to add subscription.

00:44:23.570 --> 00:44:25.070
Based on this
insight, the marketer

00:44:25.070 --> 00:44:28.850
then can target those users
by recommending them articles

00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:30.250
when they verify their email.

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:38.820
The ability to
generate these insights

00:44:38.820 --> 00:44:40.500
is where Bigtable shines.

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:42.570
Currently we have
over 12 billion events

00:44:42.570 --> 00:44:44.520
and over 200 million end users.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:47.850
The algorithm to run
this job is very join-

00:44:47.850 --> 00:44:49.860
and very shuffle-heavy.

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:52.695
However, we're now able to get
this down in under three hours.

00:44:56.020 --> 00:44:58.270
Traditionally, without
Bigtable this process

00:44:58.270 --> 00:45:00.640
would typically fetch
from the database,

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:02.980
perform some sort
ETL transformation,

00:45:02.980 --> 00:45:05.410
then store into a data warehouse
so that it doesn't effect

00:45:05.410 --> 00:45:08.410
production traffic,
then perform more ETLs,

00:45:08.410 --> 00:45:12.310
and once satisfied we would then
put it back into the database.

00:45:12.310 --> 00:45:14.890
This process is, again,
often very lengthy

00:45:14.890 --> 00:45:19.290
and requires DBA provisioning.

00:45:19.290 --> 00:45:21.510
With Bigtable, it
replaces all this.

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:23.100
As we need more
throughput, we just

00:45:23.100 --> 00:45:27.040
make an API call to
increase capacity.

00:45:27.040 --> 00:45:29.670
As Misha has
previously mentioned,

00:45:29.670 --> 00:45:31.840
Bigtable throughput
scales linearly,

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:35.800
so as we increase
node from x to y,

00:45:35.800 --> 00:45:38.340
the supported QPS
also goes from a to b.

00:45:43.250 --> 00:45:45.300
Once our Bigtable instance
has been increased

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:48.220
we are now able to run SPARC
with Cloud Dataproc over it.

00:45:51.290 --> 00:45:53.920
Once the job finishes, we
can then scale Bigtable down.

00:45:57.080 --> 00:46:01.479
Another way that we use Bigtable
is in our compute process.

00:46:01.479 --> 00:46:03.020
So our backend
infrastructure follows

00:46:03.020 --> 00:46:05.450
a service-oriented
architecture, implementing

00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:07.370
asynchronous queues.

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:10.400
And with an API call that
can vary in the workload,

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:13.030
queue backups
occasionally happen.

00:46:13.030 --> 00:46:15.250
So we implemented
our own autoscaler.

00:46:15.250 --> 00:46:18.130
It runs off of Jenkins
and watches over Datadog.

00:46:18.130 --> 00:46:21.520
It auto-scales off of queue
depth and CPU utilization,

00:46:21.520 --> 00:46:24.440
alongside with other
smaller factors.

00:46:24.440 --> 00:46:26.370
With the number of
machines it required,

00:46:26.370 --> 00:46:29.500
it will either spin up or
spin down Bigtable nodes

00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:31.660
to ensure that our
read-write latency is low.

00:46:34.857 --> 00:46:37.440
Previously, you would have to
be re-partition and add machines

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:41.910
manually to achieve
capacity, and oftentimes

00:46:41.910 --> 00:46:44.320
over-provisioning
is the right answer.

00:46:44.320 --> 00:46:46.440
However, this leads to
a higher compute cost,

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:48.600
and we're now able to
add and remove capacity

00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:50.490
with just one API call.

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:53.410
In the corner there you can see
an example of our autoscaler

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:54.860
piping the data into Slack.

00:46:58.430 --> 00:47:00.170
Products like Cloud
Bigtable are one

00:47:00.170 --> 00:47:03.590
of the reasons why startups are
able to be able to accomplish

00:47:03.590 --> 00:47:05.630
so much with such a small team.

00:47:05.630 --> 00:47:07.410
These products create
a massive leverage

00:47:07.410 --> 00:47:10.700
on the people on your team.

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:12.596
I would like to pass
it off to Misha now.

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:21.500
MISHA BRUKMAN: I
would like to thank

00:47:21.500 --> 00:47:26.860
you very much for speaking,
Robin, Wei, and Shyam.

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:30.350
This is how you can see how the
scalable products that Google

00:47:30.350 --> 00:47:33.110
Cloud Platform offers, that
it built for Google and Google

00:47:33.110 --> 00:47:36.379
services, are able to be
leveraged by companies

00:47:36.379 --> 00:47:37.920
to solve their own
big data problems.

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:40.610
So these generic
tools that we built

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:42.980
to solve our specific
problems are now

00:47:42.980 --> 00:47:45.722
possible to solve any
other problem as well

00:47:45.722 --> 00:47:46.430
in other domains.

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:49.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

